NYC Motor Vehicle Collisions Data Analysis

Project Overview

This project analyzes motor vehicle collision data in New York City to understand patterns and trends in traffic accidents. The analysis examines factors such as collision location, time, contributing factors, vehicle types, injuries, and fatalities to identify areas and conditions associated with higher collision rates.

Business Problem

The objective of this analysis is to understand the patterns and characteristics of motor vehicle collisions in New York City. The insights from the analysis can help identify high-risk locations, common contributing factors, and periods with increased collision activity, supporting better road safety planning and accident prevention.

Objectives

- Analyze the overall number of motor vehicle collisions
- Identify areas and locations with the highest number of collisions
- Analyze collisions by borough
- Identify trends in collisions over time
- Analyze collisions by month, day, and hour
- Identify the most common contributing factors
- Analyze injuries and fatalities resulting from collisions
- Identify vehicle types most frequently involved in collisions
- Identify locations that may require increased road safety attention

Tools Used

- Microsoft Excel

Dataset

The dataset contains information about motor vehicle collisions reported in New York City by the New York City Police Department. The data includes details such as collision date, collision time, borough, location, contributing factors, vehicle types, persons injured, persons killed, pedestrians injured, cyclists injured, and motorists injured.

Data Cleaning

The following data preparation steps were carried out:

- Removed duplicate records
- Handled missing and incomplete values
- Standardized borough and location information
- Corrected date and time data types
- Cleaned contributing factor categories
- Standardized vehicle type information
- Checked injury and fatality values for inconsistencies
- Created additional date and time fields for analysis
- Checked for invalid and inconsistent records
- Prepared the dataset for analysis and visualization

 Analysis

The analysis focused on:

- Total number of collisions
- Collisions by borough
- Collisions by location
- Collision trends over time
- Collisions by month
- Collisions by day of the week
- Collisions by hour
- Common contributing factors
- Injuries and fatalities
- Pedestrian, cyclist, and motorist injuries
- Vehicle types involved in collisions
- Geographic distribution of collisions

Dashboard

The final dashboard was created to provide an interactive view of motor vehicle collisions in New York City. It allows users to explore collision patterns based on factors such as location, borough, time, contributing factors, injuries, fatalities, and vehicle types.

Key Findings

- Accidents peak significantly in March (10.53%) and remain high throughout. This is typically driven by erratic early-spring weather, melting ice/slush, and fluctuating driver behavior, Collision frequencies stabilize around 7.8%−8.4% per month from April through October and Accident rates drop to their lowest levels in November (7.36%) and December (7.11%).
- Accidents occur most frequently on Fridays and during the afternoon rush hour window (3:00 PM to 6:00 PM), peaking between 4:00 PM and 5:00 PM. This aligns with high traffic volume, commuting home from work, and evening weekend travel.
- The Street where the most accidents were reported is the Belt Parkway Street reporting 3,728 accidents which represents 1.56% of all the reported accidents, followed by Broadway Street (1.17%) and Atlantic Avenue(0.94%)
- The most common contributing factor for accidents overall was seen to be "Driver Inattention/Distraction" with 58,308 accidents (24.46%) but when it comes to fatal accidents specifically, the most ontributing factor is Unsafe Speed with130 fatal accidents (21.21%) although most of the contributing factors were not specified in the dataset, so this might not be an accurate info.


Recommendations

Based on the analysis:

- Identify and prioritize high-collision locations for road safety improvements.
- Increase traffic safety awareness and enforcement in areas with high collision rates.
- Investigate common contributing factors and develop targeted measures to reduce preventable collisions.
- Consider increased monitoring during periods with higher collision activity.
- Improve road infrastructure and traffic management in areas with recurring collision incidents.
- Continue monitoring collision trends to evaluate the effectiveness of road safety interventions.

Conclusion

The analysis provides insights into motor vehicle collision patterns across New York City. By examining collision locations, time periods, contributing factors, injuries, fatalities, and vehicle types, the analysis highlights areas and circumstances associated with higher collision activity. These insights can support data-driven road safety planning and help identify opportunities to reduce collisions and their impact on road users.
